"""Chapter 3 drill items — public finance and taxation."""


def q(s, o, i, sol):
    return (s, o, i, sol)


DRILL_3 = [
    q("A public budget is best described as a statement of", ["expected revenue and planned expenditure", "the money supply and its growth", "the balance of payments", "the level of unemployment"], 0, "Revenue against expenditure for a year. The money supply belongs to monetary policy, the balance of payments to external trade, and unemployment is an outcome rather than a budget item."),
    q("A budget surplus occurs when", ["expenditure exceeds revenue", "revenue exceeds expenditure", "revenue equals expenditure", "the government borrows money"], 1, "Revenue above spending. Expenditure above revenue is a deficit, equality is a balanced budget, and borrowing is one way of financing a deficit."),
    q("A budget deficit exists when", ["government revenue exceeds expenditure", "government expenditure exceeds revenue", "the budget is balanced", "the country has a trade surplus"], 1, "Spending above revenue. The reversal of this pair — deficit and surplus — is the single most common error in the topic."),
    q("Deficit financing that is most inflationary is borrowing from", ["the public through treasury bills", "foreign governments", "the central bank", "the World Bank"], 2, "The central bank. Borrowing from it creates new money rather than transferring existing savings, so it adds directly to the money supply."),
    q("Recurrent expenditure differs from capital expenditure in that recurrent expenditure", ["creates new assets", "is spent on running costs such as salaries and fuel", "is always borrowed", "is confined to the states"], 1, "It keeps the machine running rather than building anything. Capital expenditure creates assets such as roads, schools and power stations."),
    q("Fiscal policy is the use of", ["interest rates to control the money supply", "government spending and taxation to influence the economy", "exchange controls to manage imports", "wage guidelines to control incomes"], 1, "Spending and taxation. Interest rates and the money supply are monetary policy, exchange controls are trade policy, and wage guidelines are incomes policy."),
    q("Expansionary fiscal policy is most appropriate when the economy faces", ["high inflation", "unemployment and recession", "a balance of payments surplus", "rapid growth"], 1, "Unemployment and recession, because the aim is to raise aggregate demand. Against high inflation the appropriate policy is the opposite one."),
    q("Contractionary fiscal policy would involve", ["raising taxes and cutting government spending", "cutting taxes and raising spending", "buying securities in the open market", "lowering the cash reserve ratio"], 0, "Higher taxes and lower spending both reduce aggregate demand. The other options are expansionary, and the last two are monetary rather than fiscal instruments."),
    q("An automatic stabiliser is", ["a deliberate change in tax rates", "a feature of the budget that stabilises demand without a new decision", "a central bank instrument", "a tariff imposed in a recession"], 1, "It operates without a fresh decision: unemployment benefits rise and progressive tax receipts fall automatically when incomes drop, cushioning the fall."),
    q("A direct tax is one that", ["is levied on goods and services", "is paid by the person on whom the burden finally falls", "can always be shifted forward", "is collected only at the border"], 1, "The payer bears it and cannot shift it. A tax levied on goods is indirect, and one that can be shifted is by definition indirect."),
    q("Which of the following is a direct tax?", ["value added tax", "import duty", "personal income tax", "excise duty"], 2, "Personal income tax is levied on income and cannot be passed on. VAT, import duty and excise duty are all levied on goods and are borne by consumers."),
    q("Which of the following is an indirect tax?", ["company income tax", "capital gains tax", "value added tax", "petroleum profit tax"], 2, "VAT is charged on the sale of a good and passed to the buyer. The other three are levied on profits or gains and stay where they fall."),
    q("A tax that takes a larger proportion of income from the rich than from the poor is", ["regressive", "proportional", "progressive", "neutral"], 2, "Progressive: the rate rises with income, so the share paid rises too. Proportional keeps the share constant, and regressive lets it fall."),
    q("A tax that takes the same proportion of income at every level is", ["progressive", "proportional", "regressive", "digressive"], 1, "Proportional. A flat rate on all income takes 10 per cent of ₦50,000 and 10 per cent of ₦500,000 — the same share, which is what the word means."),
    q("A tax that takes ₦10,000 from an income of ₦50,000 and ₦10,000 from an income of ₦200,000 is", ["progressive", "proportional", "regressive", "prohibitive"], 2, "Regressive: 20 per cent of the smaller income and 5 per cent of the larger. A flat amount in naira is always regressive, however it is labelled."),
    q("VAT is regarded as regressive in effect because", ["it is collected by the seller", "it takes a larger share of a poor household's spending", "it is levied only on luxury goods", "it varies with the taxpayer's income"], 1, "Poor households spend a larger fraction of their income on goods, so the same rate bites harder. Who collects it is irrelevant to its incidence."),
    q("The principle that a tax should be based on ability to pay is", ["certainty", "economy", "equity", "convenience"], 2, "Equity. Certainty is about knowing what is owed, economy about collection costs, and convenience about the time and manner of payment."),
    q("Pay-as-you-earn taxation illustrates the principle of", ["equity", "certainty", "convenience", "economy"], 2, "Convenience: the tax is deducted at the moment the income is received, which suits the payer and reduces evasion."),
    q("The Laffer curve suggests that beyond a certain point, raising the tax rate", ["raises revenue proportionally", "reduces total revenue", "has no effect on revenue", "reduces the price level"], 1, "Revenue falls, because work, trade and investment move out of the taxed sector. That is the whole content of the curve."),
    q("Tax incidence refers to", ["the legal obligation to pay a tax", "who finally bears the economic burden of a tax", "the rate at which a tax is levied", "the cost of collecting a tax"], 1, "Who bears it. The legal payer and the economic bearer are frequently different people, and the difference is what incidence studies."),
    q("The burden of a tax falls most heavily on the side of the market that is", ["more elastic", "less elastic", "larger in number", "better informed"], 1, "Less elastic, because that side cannot change quantity to escape the tax. Elasticity, not the statute, decides who pays."),
    q("If demand is perfectly inelastic, a tax on the good is borne", ["entirely by producers", "entirely by consumers", "equally by both", "by the government"], 1, "Entirely by consumers: they will pay any price for the same quantity, so the whole tax passes forward into the price."),
    q("If supply is perfectly inelastic, a tax on the good is borne", ["entirely by producers", "entirely by consumers", "equally by both", "by importers"], 0, "Entirely by producers, who cannot reduce quantity in response. This is the classic case of a fixed factor, such as land."),
    q("The public debt is best described as", ["the deficit of the current year", "the accumulated total of past deficits", "the money owed to the central bank only", "the country's external reserves"], 1, "The accumulated stock of past borrowing. The deficit is the annual flow that adds to it, and reserves are an asset rather than a liability."),
    q("Debt service refers to", ["the total size of the debt", "the interest and principal paid in a year", "the borrowing undertaken in a year", "the rescheduling of a loan"], 1, "The payments due in the year — interest plus principal. It is the part of the debt that competes with schools and roads in the budget."),
    q("External debt differs from domestic debt in that external debt", ["is always cheaper", "is denominated in foreign currency", "is owed to citizens", "never has to be repaid"], 1, "It is owed in foreign currency, so repayment requires foreign exchange and a depreciation raises its domestic cost."),
    q("Crowding out occurs when government borrowing", ["reduces the interest rate", "raises the interest rate and reduces private investment", "increases private investment", "lowers the exchange rate"], 1, "Government demand for credit pushes interest rates up, which prices private borrowers out of the market — the standard criticism of deficit financing domestically."),
    q("Which of the following is a source of government revenue?", ["imports", "company income tax", "unemployment", "inflation"], 1, "Company income tax is a receipt. Imports, unemployment and inflation are economic conditions, not revenue heads, although imports generate customs duty."),
    q("A government wishing to reduce a budget deficit should", ["cut taxes and raise spending", "raise taxes or cut spending", "print more money", "borrow more from abroad"], 1, "Raise revenue or cut expenditure. Printing money and borrowing widen the problem by adding to the money supply or to the debt stock."),
    q("The main purpose of taxation is to", ["control inflation directly", "raise revenue for government expenditure", "redistribute income only", "reduce imports"], 1, "Raise revenue. Redistribution and the regulation of behaviour are secondary purposes, and inflation control is a monetary matter."),
    q("A tax on a commodity whose demand is elastic will", ["raise a large revenue", "raise little revenue and reduce the quantity sold sharply", "have no effect on quantity", "be borne entirely by producers without any change in quantity"], 1, "Little revenue, because the quantity falls sharply when the price rises. Revenue is largest where demand is inelastic."),
    q("Progressive taxation is defended mainly on the ground that it", ["is easy to collect", "reduces inequality by taking a larger share from higher incomes", "raises more revenue than any other system", "encourages work"], 1, "It reduces inequality. Ease of collection, revenue yield and incentives are separate arguments and are often made against progressivity rather than for it."),
    q("Which is a capital receipt of government?", ["proceeds from the sale of government shares in a company", "company income tax", "value added tax", "customs duty"], 0, "Proceeds from selling an asset are a capital receipt. The three taxes are recurrent revenue, which is collected year after year."),
    q("Public finance is concerned with", ["the finances of private firms", "the revenue and expenditure of government", "the profits of commercial banks", "household budgeting"], 1, "Government revenue, expenditure and debt. The others are private finance, which is studied separately."),
    q("A tariff is best classified as", ["a direct tax on income", "an indirect tax on imports", "a subsidy to exporters", "a form of government spending"], 1, "An indirect tax levied at the border and borne by consumers of the imported good, which is also why it is protection as well as revenue."),
    q("When a government raises a tax on producers in a market where demand is elastic, the main effect is", ["a large rise in price with little change in quantity", "a small rise in price with a large fall in quantity", "no change in either", "a fall in price"], 1, "Producers absorb most of the tax, so the price rises little and the quantity falls sharply — the opposite of the inelastic case."),
    q("The fiscal year in Nigeria runs from", ["January to December", "April to March", "July to June", "October to September"], 0, "January to December, which matters when a question dates a budget or a revenue figure."),
    q("Which combination describes a country in the strongest fiscal position?", ["large deficit, large debt service", "budget surplus, falling debt as a share of GDP", "balanced budget, rising borrowing", "deficit financed by the central bank"], 1, "A surplus with a falling debt ratio. The others either add to the debt or finance it in the most inflationary way available."),
    q("Tax evasion differs from tax avoidance in that evasion", ["is legal", "is illegal", "is a form of avoidance", "applies only to companies"], 1, "Evasion is illegal — not declaring income or falsifying returns. Avoidance uses legal means to reduce the liability, and the distinction is a favourite question."),
    q("Public goods are those which", ["are provided free of charge by the state", "are non-excludable and non-rival, so the market under-provides them", "are consumed only by the poor", "are produced by public corporations"], 1, "Non-excludable and non-rival: no one can be kept out and one person's use does not reduce another's, so private sellers cannot charge and the state must provide."),
]
